"""Grade 12: vocation, the faith in the modern world and the last things, written as a catechist would plan it."""
R = {
'12-1-1': dict(
 idea='God calls every person to holiness and to a particular way of life. Vocation begins with Baptism and unfolds through discernment: prayer, the sacraments, the Church’s guidance and honest self-knowledge. Jeremiah’s call shows that God knows and chooses us before we do.',
 hook='Read Jeremiah 1:4–8 aloud. Ask: “What excuse does Jeremiah give? What does God answer?” Students name excuses they use when something feels too big.',
 activity='“Vocation journal” (40 min). Students start a four-part journal: gifts (what I do well), joys (what gives me life), needs (what the world needs that I notice) and invitations (moments I sensed a call). They write a first entry on each and name one trusted person to discuss it with.',
 teach=['All are called to holiness (CCC 825, 2013; Lumen Gentium 40). Each also has a particular vocation within the one mission of the Church (CCC 1877–1879, 897–933).',
        'God’s call is personal: “Before I formed you in the womb I knew you” (Jeremiah 1:5). It comes through gifts, circumstances, desires purified by prayer and the Church’s discernment.',
        'Discernment uses Ignatian tools: notice consolations and desolations, pray with Scripture, seek spiritual direction, test in action (CCC 2690).',
        'Reassure students: God does not hide his will; a vocation is a gift to be received, not a puzzle to solve alone.'],
 misc=['“A vocation means the priesthood or religious life.”', '“If I choose wrong, I have ruined God’s plan.”'],
 mem='“Before I formed you in the womb I knew you, and before you were born I consecrated you.” (Jeremiah 1:5)',
 assess='Ask students to explain the difference between the universal call to holiness and a particular vocation, and name one step in discernment.'),
'12-1-2': dict(
 idea='The Church recognizes four ways of living out the call to holiness: marriage, the ordained priesthood and diaconate, consecrated life, and single life lived for God. Each is a real vocation with its own graces and challenges, and each needs the others.',
 hook='Read 1 Corinthians 7:7: “Each has his own gift from God.” Ask: “Why would Paul say that both marriage and celibacy are gifts?”',
 activity='“Interview someone in each vocation” (45 min). Over a week, students interview a married person, a priest or deacon, a consecrated religious and a committed single person. They ask: How did you know? What is the hardest part? What surprised you? Present findings in a one-page comparison.',
 teach=['Marriage is a covenant and a path of holiness for the couple and children (CCC 1601–1666). Holy Orders configures a man to Christ the head and servant (CCC 1536–1600).',
        'Consecrated life lives the evangelical counsels of poverty, chastity and obedience as a sign of the Kingdom (CCC 914–933). Single life can be a gift of availability when chosen for God, and also a circumstance that is lived in faith.',
        'None is “higher” in dignity; each is higher in what God asks of that person (CCC 1620). Vatican II teaches a universal call to holiness.',
        'Pray for vocations. Discernment is communal as well as personal.'],
 misc=['“Marriage is the default; priesthood and religious life are rare exceptions.”', '“Single people have not been called.”'],
 mem='“Each has his own special gift from God, one of one kind and one of another.” (1 Corinthians 7:7)',
 assess='Ask students to describe each of the four states of life and what each witnesses to.'),
'12-1-3': dict(
 idea='Work is part of God’s plan: humans share in his creative work and sanctify the world by it. A vocation includes how we use our gifts for others. Work has dignity, and workers have rights.',
 hook='Read Genesis 2:15 (“to till it and keep it”). Ask: “Was work a punishment or a gift?” Work existed before the fall; toil was added by sin.',
 activity='“Reflect on a career” (40 min). Students write about a career or field they are considering, answering: How could this serve others? What would it ask of me morally? What could go wrong? Where could I be a Christian witness? Pair up and share.',
 teach=['Work is a duty and a way to participate in God’s creative work (CCC 2427). Laborem Exercens (John Paul II): work is “for man,” not man for work.',
        'Rights of workers: just wage, safe conditions, rest, the right to organize (CCC 2433–2436). Employers have duties of justice (CCC 2434).',
        'Rest is part of God’s plan: Sabbath (CCC 2184–2188). Work must not become an idol.',
        'Vocation includes how we love in daily work: “Whatever your task, work heartily, as serving the Lord” (Colossians 3:23). St Joseph the Worker is a model.'],
 misc=['“Work is a result of sin.”', '“My job is just a way to make money.”'],
 mem='“Whatever you do, work heartily, as for the Lord and not for men.” (Colossians 3:23)',
 assess='Ask students to explain a Christian view of work and apply it to a career path.'),
'12-2-1': dict(
 idea='Faith and reason are two wings by which the human spirit rises to the truth (Fides et Ratio). Because God is the author of both, genuine science and faith cannot conflict. When they seem to, we have misread one of them.',
 hook='Show the opening line of Fides et Ratio: “Faith and reason are like two wings.” Ask: “What happens to a bird with one wing?”',
 activity='“Study Fides et Ratio” (45 min). Students read excerpts from John Paul II’s encyclical (1998) and summarize: the dangers of fideism and rationalism, the role of philosophy, and the call to seek truth. Then take a case (Galileo, evolution, the Big Bang and Fr. Lemaître) and show how faith and science interact.',
 teach=['“Even though faith is above reason, there can never be any real discrepancy between faith and reason” (CCC 159). The same God who reveals mysteries has endowed the human spirit with reason.',
        'Methodical research in every branch of knowledge, when carried out in a truly scientific manner, “never conflicts with faith” (CCC 159; Gaudium et Spes 36).',
        'Fr. Georges Lemaître, a Catholic priest, proposed the Big Bang theory. Gregor Mendel, an Augustinian friar, founded genetics. Many scientists have been believers.',
        'Scientism, the claim that only empirical science yields truth, is itself a philosophical position, not a scientific result.'],
 misc=['“Faith is opposed to science.”', '“The Church fears scientific discovery.”'],
 mem='“The heavens declare the glory of God, and the firmament proclaims his handiwork.” (Psalm 19:1)',
 assess='Ask students to explain how faith and reason relate and respond to an alleged conflict.'),
'12-2-2': dict(
 idea='The Church engages unbelief with honesty and charity. Atheism takes many forms: practical atheism, agnosticism, scientism, and secularism. The Church listens, asks why, and offers the reasons for hope. Believers also bear some responsibility for unbelief.',
 hook='Read Acts 17:22–31. Ask: “How does Paul begin his speech to the Athenians? What does he start from?” He starts from their own search.',
 activity='“Debate” (45 min). Teams argue the question “Can one be good without God?” One side takes a Christian view of grounding morality in God; the other presents a secular case and its strengths. Afterwards they list where they found common ground and where the real disagreement lies.',
 teach=['Atheism is one of the gravest problems of our time (CCC 2123–2126; Gaudium et Spes 19–21). It has diverse forms: denial of God, agnosticism, indifference, scientism, ideologies.',
        'Believers can contribute to unbelief when they present a distorted image of God (CCC 2125). A credible witness and genuine dialogue are needed.',
        'Secularism separates public life from faith; the Church distinguishes healthy secularity (autonomy of earthly affairs) from secularism (excluding God).',
        'Engage with respect. Listen to what people have suffered or doubted. Offer reasons: the contingency of the world, the moral law, beauty, Christ.'],
 misc=['“Atheists are immoral.”', '“Faith is irrational.”'],
 mem='“Always be prepared to make a defense to anyone who calls you to account for the hope that is in you; yet do it with gentleness and reverence.” (1 Peter 3:15)',
 assess='Ask students to describe three forms of unbelief and how to engage each charitably.'),
'12-2-3': dict(
 idea='The Church affirms religious freedom as a right rooted in human dignity: no one may be forced to act against conscience or prevented from acting in accord with it, within just limits. Faith must be free, and the Church defends this freedom for all people.',
 hook='Read John 18:36 (“My kingdom is not of this world”). Ask: “Why did Jesus refuse to fight?” The Gospel is proposed, not imposed.',
 activity='“Study Dignitatis Humanae” (45 min). Students read key paragraphs (DH 2–4) and summarize the argument. They then map current cases of religious persecution and restriction around the world and discuss what believers and governments should do.',
 teach=['Vatican II’s Dignitatis Humanae (1965): the right to religious freedom is based on the dignity of the human person (CCC 2104–2109). Faith is a free act and cannot be coerced.',
        'This right is for everyone, including non-Christians. The state must protect it, within just public order. The Church also claims freedom to carry out her mission.',
        'The teaching develops earlier practice: the Church acknowledges that coercion in matters of faith was sometimes wrongly used in history.',
        'Religious freedom is increasingly threatened: Christians are among the most persecuted groups worldwide.'],
 misc=['“Religious freedom means all religions are equally true.”', '“The Church always supported religious freedom.”'],
 mem='“My kingdom is not of this world.” (John 18:36)',
 assess='Ask students to explain the foundation of religious freedom and one global application.'),
'12-3-1': dict(
 idea='Subsidiarity holds that decisions should be made at the lowest level able to handle them, with higher bodies helping but not replacing. Participation means every person has the right and duty to contribute to the common good. These two principles protect freedom and community.',
 hook='Read 1 Corinthians 12:14–20. Ask: “What if the head did everything and the hands did nothing?” Introduce subsidiarity.',
 activity='“Case study” (40 min). Provide a scenario (a school district, a parish, a city) where a central authority has taken over local decisions, or where local groups are neglecting a need. Students apply subsidiarity: who should decide? Who should help? Where is intervention justified?',
 teach=['Subsidiarity: “a community of a higher order should not interfere in the internal life of a community of a lower order, depriving the latter of its functions” (CCC 1883; Pius XI, Quadragesimo Anno).',
        'It also requires higher bodies to help when lower ones cannot cope (CCC 1884–1885). It guards against both individualism and collectivism.',
        'Participation: all citizens have a right and duty to take part in public life (CCC 1913–1917). Voting, serving, volunteering, informed engagement.',
        'Show examples: the family before the state, the parish before the diocese, local charity before national programs.'],
 misc=['“Subsidiarity means less government always.”', '“Participation is only voting.”'],
 mem='“Just as the body is one and has many members… so it is with Christ.” (1 Corinthians 12:12)',
 assess='Ask students to define subsidiarity and apply it to a concrete case.'),
'12-3-2': dict(
 idea='Economic life exists to serve the person. The Church teaches the dignity of work, a just wage, the right to private property with a social purpose, the rights of workers to organize, and care for the poor. Rerum Novarum began a long tradition of social teaching on economics.',
 hook='Read James 5:1–6 on wages held back. Ask: “What does James condemn? What does it say about how God sees workers?”',
 activity='“Reading Rerum Novarum summary” (45 min). Students read a summary of Leo XIII’s 1891 encyclical. They identify: what it rejects (socialism’s abolition of property, unregulated capitalism), what it affirms (private property, a just wage, unions). Then apply to a modern case: gig work, minimum wage, remote work.',
 teach=['Work is for the person; the economy must serve people (CCC 2426–2428). Economic life is subject to moral law (CCC 2423–2425).',
        'Just wages enable a family to live with dignity (CCC 2434). Workers have the right to strike in just circumstances and to form unions (CCC 2435).',
        'Private property is legitimate but has a “universal destination of goods” (CCC 2402–2406). Charity does not replace justice.',
        'Later documents (Quadragesimo Anno, Centesimus Annus, Caritas in Veritate) address globalization, markets and the common good.'],
 misc=['“The Church endorses socialism.”', '“The Church endorses unrestrained capitalism.”'],
 mem='“Behold, the wages of the laborers who mowed your fields, which you kept back by fraud, cry out; and the cries of the harvesters have reached the ears of the Lord of hosts.” (James 5:4)',
 assess='Ask students to state four principles of Catholic economic teaching.'),
'12-3-3': dict(
 idea='The Church promotes peace as “the tranquility of order” and holds that war is always a failure of humanity. She also teaches that use of force may be legitimate in strictly limited conditions of defense, when just war criteria are met. Peacemakers are blessed.',
 hook='Read Matthew 5:9: “Blessed are the peacemakers.” Ask: “Is peace the same as the absence of war?”',
 activity='“Study” (45 min). Students study the just war criteria (CCC 2309): lasting, grave damage by an aggressor; all other means impractical; serious prospects of success; no greater evils produced. They then evaluate a historical case against the criteria and discuss the role of conscientious objectors.',
 teach=['Peace is “the tranquility of order” (Augustine; CCC 2304) and requires justice and charity. “Blessed are the peacemakers” (CCC 2305).',
        'All citizens and governments are obliged to avoid war; yet governments have the right of legitimate defense (CCC 2308–2309). The conditions for just war are strict; the means must also be just (CCC 2312–2314).',
        'Conscientious objection is respected (CCC 2311). Arms race, terrorism, torture and indiscriminate destruction are gravely wrong (CCC 2314–2317).',
        'Recent popes have asked whether war can still be justified given modern weapons. The Church supports nonviolent peacemaking.'],
 misc=['“The Church is pacifist.”', '“Just war means any war our side declares just.”'],
 mem='“Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God.” (Matthew 5:9)',
 assess='Ask students to list the just war criteria and apply them to a case.'),
'12-4-1': dict(
 idea='Every human life is sacred from conception to natural death because each person is made in God’s image. Direct abortion and the intentional killing of the innocent are gravely wrong, and the Church calls for help for mothers, families and everyone in need.',
 hook='Read Psalm 139:13–16. Ask: “What does this say about when a person begins to matter to God?”',
 activity='“Research” (45 min). Students research the science of fetal development, local pregnancy-help services and Church teaching (Evangelium Vitae). They produce a one-page brief that presents the Church’s argument, how to respond to common objections, and ways to support women in crisis. Respect those with painful experiences.',
 teach=['Human life is sacred from conception until natural death (CCC 2258, 2270–2271). Abortion is a grave moral evil (CCC 2271); formal cooperation incurs excommunication (CCC 2272).',
        'Science: a unique human organism with its own DNA begins at fertilization. The Church’s argument is based on human dignity, not only on faith.',
        'The Church also teaches compassion: Project Rachel offers healing for post-abortion pain; pregnancy centers and parishes can support women (CCC 2274–2275).',
        'Respect for life also includes the poor, migrants, the disabled and the elderly (Evangelium Vitae 87).'],
 misc=['“The Church only cares about unborn life.”', '“Abortion is a purely religious position.”'],
 mem='“For you formed my inward parts; you knitted me together in my mother’s womb.” (Psalm 139:13)',
 assess='Ask students to present the Church’s case and how to support women in crisis.'),
'12-4-2': dict(
 idea='Euthanasia and assisted suicide are morally unacceptable because they directly end a life. The Church also allows refusing burdensome treatment and strongly supports palliative care. Suffering has meaning in Christ, and the dying deserve accompaniment.',
 hook='Read John 11, the raising of Lazarus. Ask: “What does Jesus do at the tomb? What does he feel?” He weeps. The Church does not minimize suffering.',
 activity='“Case studies” (45 min). Present three cases: a terminally ill patient requesting assisted suicide, a family deciding on withdrawing ventilators, a hospice patient receiving palliative sedation. Students analyze using the distinction between ordinary and extraordinary means and between intending death and accepting it.',
 teach=['Euthanasia — an action or omission that of itself or by intention causes death in order to eliminate suffering — is morally unacceptable (CCC 2277). Assisted suicide is also gravely wrong (CCC 2282).',
        'Discontinuing disproportionate or burdensome treatment is legitimate (CCC 2278). Pain relief, even if it shortens life, is acceptable if death is not intended (CCC 2279).',
        'Palliative care is a preferred form of charity. Hospice care honors the dying.',
        'Suffering is not good, but united to Christ’s Passion it can be redemptive (CCC 1521, 1505). Accompany the dying with love, presence and the sacraments.'],
 misc=['“The Church requires keeping people alive at all costs.”', '“Suffering has no meaning.”'],
 mem='“Jesus wept.” (John 11:35)',
 assess='Ask students to distinguish euthanasia from refusal of burdensome treatment with examples.'),
'12-4-3': dict(
 idea='The Church engages new technologies in medicine, biology and computing. She asks of each: Does it respect the dignity of the person? Is the person treated as a subject or as an object? The Church supports science that heals and opposes what degrades human life.',
 hook='Read Wisdom 9:1–6, the prayer for wisdom. Ask: “What does Solomon ask for? Why is wisdom needed with power?”',
 activity='“Debate” (45 min). Teams debate a motion: “Gene editing of human embryos should be banned.” Use principles: dignity, the common good, the difference between therapy and enhancement. Consider related topics: artificial intelligence, organ donation, stem cells. Write a guiding question to ask of any new technology.',
 teach=['Scientific research is a noble service when it respects human dignity (CCC 2292–2296). Experiments on human beings must respect their rights and consent.',
        'Organ donation is praiseworthy when free and not harmful (CCC 2296). Embryonic stem cell research is wrong since it destroys embryos; adult stem cell research is licit.',
        'Reproductive technologies that separate procreation from the marital act are wrong (CCC 2376–2377). Gene therapy for healing is allowed; eugenics and enhancement raise grave questions.',
        'Artificial intelligence and data raise questions of privacy, work and the human person (Rome Call for AI Ethics; Antiqua et Nova).'],
 misc=['“The Church is against all new technology.”', '“If we can do it, we should.”'],
 mem='“Give me wisdom, that sits by your throne.” (Wisdom 9:4)',
 assess='Ask students to propose three questions to ask of any new technology.'),
'12-5-1': dict(
 idea='The Orthodox Churches and the Eastern Catholic Churches are very close to the Catholic Church: they share apostolic succession, valid sacraments, the Creed and devotion to Mary and the saints. The main differences concern papal primacy and jurisdiction, the Filioque and some doctrines.',
 hook='Read John 17. Ask: “What does Jesus pray for? How might the Churches heed that?”',
 activity='“Visit an Eastern church” (60 min). Attend a Divine Liturgy (or watch a recording). Students note: the iconostasis, incense, chant, the priest’s role, the epiclesis, communion with leavened bread. Afterwards they list what felt familiar and what was new, and write a note of thanks.',
 teach=['The Eastern Churches possess true sacraments, especially the priesthood and Eucharist (CCC 838, 1399–1400; Unitatis Redintegratio 14–18). Catholics may receive Communion in Orthodox churches in some circumstances when no Catholic ministers are available, as the Code of Canon Law provides.',
        'Differences: authority of the Pope, the Filioque, purgatory, the Immaculate Conception, divorce and remarriage.',
        'Eastern Catholic Churches are in full communion with the Pope, with their own rites, canon law and married priests.',
        'Pope John Paul II’s “two lungs” image and Ut Unum Sint call for continued dialogue.'],
 misc=['“Orthodox and Catholics are totally different religions.”', '“Reunion would require one side to surrender.”'],
 mem='“That they may all be one.” (John 17:21)',
 assess='Ask students to state three points of agreement and two of difference between Catholic and Orthodox.'),
'12-5-2': dict(
 idea='Protestant communities share with Catholics Baptism, Scripture and faith in Christ, but differ on authority, the sacraments, ministry and justification. The Church speaks with respect and works for unity.',
 hook='Read Ephesians 4:1–6. Ask: “What does Paul call us to?” Unity in one body.',
 activity='“Study Unitatis Redintegratio” (45 min). Students read UR 19–24 and summarize what it says about Protestant communities: elements of truth, genuine Christian life, differences, and the invitation to dialogue. Compare the Joint Declaration on Justification (1999) with Reformation-era disputes.',
 teach=['Those who are baptized and believe in Christ are in a certain, though imperfect, communion with the Catholic Church (CCC 818–819). Their communities possess elements of sanctification and truth.',
        'Differences: sola scriptura vs. Scripture and Tradition; papal authority; the Eucharist; Mary and the saints; seven sacraments vs. two or three.',
        'Ecumenism includes prayer, theological dialogue, shared service. The Church recognizes Protestant Baptism if properly done with water and the Trinitarian formula (CCC 1256).',
        'Avoid proselytizing; witness with charity and clarity.'],
 misc=['“Protestants are not Christians.”', '“All differences are merely semantic.”'],
 mem='“There is one body and one Spirit… one Lord, one faith, one baptism.” (Ephesians 4:4–5)',
 assess='Ask students to explain common ground with Protestants and two real differences.'),
'12-5-3': dict(
 idea='The Church honors the Jewish people as the people of the first covenant, esteems Muslims who worship the one God, and respects what is true and holy in other religions. She calls Catholics to dialogue and to reject all anti-Semitism and hatred.',
 hook='Read Romans 11:1–2, 11:29. Ask: “What does Paul say about God’s gifts to Israel?” “Irrevocable.”',
 activity='“Interreligious conversation” (60 min). Invite guests or visit a synagogue, mosque, or other house of worship. Prepare respectful questions. Afterward reflect: what did we share? What distinguishes our faith? How can we cooperate for good?',
 teach=['The Church recognizes the Jewish people as God’s first-called people. “To them belong the sonship, the glory, the covenants” (Romans 9:4). Anti-Semitism is a sin against the Gospel (CCC 839–840; Nostra Aetate 4).',
        'Muslims profess the faith of Abraham and worship the one merciful God. We share much, though we differ about Christ and the Trinity (CCC 841).',
        'Other religions contain rays of truth (CCC 843–845). The Church’s mission remains to proclaim Christ, with respect for freedom.',
        'Catholic–Jewish and Catholic–Muslim dialogues continue.'],
 misc=['“Jews were replaced by the Church.”', '“All religions say the same thing.”'],
 mem='“For the gifts and the call of God are irrevocable.” (Romans 11:29)',
 assess='Ask students to explain the Church’s attitude toward Jews, Muslims and other religions.'),
'12-6-1': dict(
 idea='Sola scriptura (“Scripture alone”) is the claim that the Bible is the sole authority. Catholics respond with Scripture and history: Scripture itself points to Tradition and the Church, the canon was set by the Church, and a pillar of truth is needed to settle disputes.',
 hook='Ask: “Where does the Bible say ‘the Bible alone’?” Open 2 Timothy 3:16 and read carefully. Ask what it says and does not say.',
 activity='“Use the Apologetics section” (45 min). Students use the Apologetics section of the app. For each of three objections (“sola scriptura is biblical”, “traditions of men”, “Catholic traditions contradict the Bible”), they gather verses (2 Thessalonians 2:15; 1 Timothy 3:15; 2 Peter 1:20; Matthew 15:3–9) and historical facts (the canon) and write a concise reply.',
 teach=['Scripture and Tradition together form the deposit of faith (CCC 80–82). 2 Thessalonians 2:15 commends oral and written traditions. 1 Timothy 3:15 calls the Church “the pillar and bulwark of the truth.”',
        '2 Timothy 3:16–17 says Scripture is “profitable,” not that it is “sufficient alone.” The canon itself was determined by the Church’s Tradition.',
        'Jesus condemned human traditions that contradict God’s commands (Mark 7:8), not apostolic tradition.',
        'Be fair: Protestants hold to the high authority of Scripture and many regard creeds and confessions as authoritative. Dialogue calmly.'],
 misc=['“Catholics don’t read the Bible.”', '“Tradition means adding to Scripture.”'],
 mem='“Stand firm and hold to the traditions that you were taught by us, either by our spoken word or by our letter.” (2 Thessalonians 2:15)',
 assess='Ask students to give a concise reply to sola scriptura with two Scriptural and one historical point.'),
'12-6-2': dict(
 idea='The Real Presence and the intercession of the saints are two doctrines often challenged. The biblical and patristic evidence for them is strong: John 6, the words of institution, 1 Corinthians 10–11, and the early Church’s practice of honoring martyrs.',
 hook='Ask: “If you were to prove to a skeptic that Christians have always believed in the Real Presence, what would you show?” List the sources.',
 activity='“Practice” (45 min). Pairs role-play a friendly conversation: one raises an objection (“Isn’t communion just a symbol?”; “Why pray to saints?”), the other responds with Scripture (John 6:53–56; 1 Corinthians 11:27–29; Revelation 5:8) and Fathers (Ignatius, Justin, Cyril of Jerusalem). Switch roles and give feedback on tone.',
 teach=['The Church believes in the Real Presence on the basis of Christ’s words and the unanimous witness of the early Church (CCC 1374–1376). Ignatius (c. 107): “the Eucharist is the flesh of our Savior Jesus Christ.”',
        'The saints intercede for us (CCC 956–957; Revelation 5:8, 6:9–10). The early Church honored martyrs’ tombs and asked for their prayers (catacomb inscriptions).',
        'Avoid proof-texting. Show the whole pattern of Scripture and Tradition.',
        'Always remember the person: a good answer is gentle and patient.'],
 misc=['“Early Christians saw the Eucharist as a symbol.”', '“Intercession of saints is necromancy.”'],
 mem='“Truly, truly, I say to you, unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink his blood, you have no life in you.” (John 6:53)',
 assess='Ask students to present a two-minute answer to an objection about the Eucharist or saints.'),
'12-6-3': dict(
 idea='Two common objections concern Confession (“Why go to a priest?”) and the papacy (“Why is Peter the rock?”). John 20:21–23 and Matthew 16:13–19 are central, and the early Church’s practice supports the Catholic understanding.',
 hook='Read John 20:21–23 and Matthew 16:18–19. Ask: “What power does Jesus give? To whom?”',
 activity='“Role-play” (45 min). In trios (questioner, responder, observer), students practice answering: “Only God can forgive sins” and “Peter is just a pebble (petros) not the rock (petra).” Use Mark 2:7–10, John 20:23, the Aramaic “Kepha,” Isaiah 22:22, and early testimonies (Clement, Ignatius, Irenaeus, Cyprian). Observers give feedback.',
 teach=['Christ gave the apostles the power to forgive sins in his name (John 20:21–23; CCC 1441–1442). “God has reconciled us to himself and given us the ministry of reconciliation” (2 Corinthians 5:18).',
        'The papacy: Peter is the rock (Matthew 16:18), receives the keys (Isaiah 22:22; Matthew 16:19), confirms his brothers (Luke 22:32) and feeds the flock (John 21:15–17) (CCC 881–882).',
        'In Aramaic Jesus said “Kepha” for both Peter and the rock. Early Fathers acknowledge Rome’s special authority (Irenaeus, Against Heresies 3.3).',
        'Present the doctrine of papal infallibility accurately (see earlier grades).'],
 misc=['“The priest forgives by his own authority.”', '“The papacy is a medieval invention.”'],
 mem='“Receive the Holy Spirit. If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven them.” (John 20:22–23)',
 assess='Ask students to answer one objection about Confession and one about the papacy.'),
'12-7-1': dict(
 idea='Death ends our earthly pilgrimage. Each person is judged by Christ immediately after death (particular judgment) and again at the end of time (general judgment). Hebrews 9:27: “It is appointed for men to die once, and after that comes judgment.” This is a call to live now with love.',
 hook='Ask: “If you had one year to live, what would change?” Take answers. Then read Hebrews 9:27.',
 activity='“Reflect” (40 min). Students write a reflective piece on the Church’s teaching on death and judgment, ending with a short examination of conscience and a list of what matters most. Offer silent prayer and, if desired, the Prayer for a Happy Death.',
 teach=['Death is the end of earthly life and the entrance to eternal life (CCC 1005–1014). Christ transformed death: for believers, it is a participation in his death and resurrection (CCC 1006–1009).',
        'The particular judgment: “Each man receives his eternal retribution in his immortal soul at the very moment of his death” (CCC 1022). The general judgment will reveal all (CCC 1038–1041).',
        'We are judged on love (Matthew 25). Mercy and justice meet in Christ (CCC 679).',
        'Remember to speak gently with students who have lost loved ones. Memento mori is spiritual practice, not morbid fear.'],
 misc=['“Reincarnation is compatible with Christian faith.”', '“God judges harshly.”'],
 mem='“It is appointed for men to die once, and after that comes judgment.” (Hebrews 9:27)',
 assess='Ask students to explain the particular and general judgment and how they shape life now.'),
'12-7-2': dict(
 idea='After death, those who die in God’s grace and friendship and are perfectly purified live forever with him (heaven). Those who die in God’s friendship but need purification undergo purgatory. Those who freely and finally refuse him choose hell. The Church knows these as realities, not as maps.',
 hook='Read Luke 16:19–31, the rich man and Lazarus. Ask: “What does the story warn about? What does it show about ongoing relationship?”',
 activity='“Study” (45 min). Students read CCC 1023–1037 and compose a three-column summary: heaven (vision of God, communion), purgatory (purification, prayer for the dead, 2 Maccabees 12:45), hell (self-exclusion, the reality of freedom). Add a pastoral reflection on how the Church prays for the dead.',
 teach=['Heaven is “the ultimate end and fulfillment of the deepest human longings” (CCC 1024). The blessed see God face to face (CCC 1023–1029).',
        'Purgatory is a final purification (CCC 1030–1032). The Church prays for the dead and offers Masses, alms and indulgences for them.',
        'Hell is a state of definitive self-exclusion from communion with God (CCC 1033–1037). God predestines no one to hell. The Church canonizes saints but never declares anyone damned.',
        'Rather than speculation, the Church stresses hope and conversion.'],
 misc=['“Hell is God’s torture chamber.”', '“Purgatory is a second chance after death.”'],
 mem='“Eye has not seen, nor ear heard, nor the heart of man imagined, what God has prepared for those who love him.” (1 Corinthians 2:9)',
 assess='Ask students to explain heaven, purgatory and hell and their pastoral implications.'),
'12-7-3': dict(
 idea='Scripture ends with the promise of new heavens and a new earth where God dwells with his people. Creation will be transformed and the body will rise. Christian hope is not escape from the world but its renewal.',
 hook='Read Revelation 21:1–5. Ask: “What is new? What is missing?” The story ends with a city and a garden.',
 activity='“Meditation” (40 min). Students pray Revelation 21 slowly, imagining the scene. They list images and connect each to Genesis (tree of life, river). They write a short prayer of hope and reflect on how this vision affects work for justice and care for creation now.',
 teach=['At the end the universe will be renewed: “a new heaven and a new earth” (CCC 1042–1050; Revelation 21:1). Our bodies will rise glorified (CCC 988–1004).',
        'The vision of God, communion of saints and life with the Trinity are the fulfilment of human desire (CCC 1023–1029).',
        'Christian hope motivates work in the world: “earthly progress must be carefully distinguished from the growth of Christ’s kingdom” yet it can contribute (CCC 1049).',
        'The liturgy anticipates it: the Mass joins heaven and earth.'],
 misc=['“We will float on clouds forever.”', '“The material world will be destroyed and forgotten.”'],
 mem='“Behold, I am making all things new.” (Revelation 21:5)',
 assess='Ask students to describe the new creation and how it influences present action.'),
'12-8-1': dict(
 idea='Laypeople are the Church in the world. Through Baptism they share in Christ’s priestly, prophetic and kingly mission, and their special vocation is to sanctify the world from within: in family, work, culture and public life.',
 hook='Read Matthew 5:13–16 (salt and light). Ask: “Where are you called to be salt and light this year?”',
 activity='“Write a mission statement” (45 min). Students write a one-page personal mission statement for their lay vocation: values, relationships, work, parish, service. They name three concrete commitments and a person who will hold them accountable.',
 teach=['The laity “seek the kingdom of God by engaging in temporal affairs and directing them according to God’s will” (CCC 898; Lumen Gentium 31). Apostolicam Actuositatem (the Decree on the Lay Apostolate) elaborates this.',
        'They share in Christ’s priestly, prophetic and kingly office (CCC 901–913). They can also serve in parish ministries.',
        'Examples: St Thomas More (politics), St Gianna Beretta Molla (medicine, motherhood), Blessed Frédéric Ozanam (charity), St Josemaría Escrivá (sanctifying work).',
        'Avoid clericalizing the laity. Their mission belongs to them by Baptism.'],
 misc=['“The laity’s role is to pray, pay and obey.”', '“Politics and faith should never mix.”'],
 mem='“Let your light shine before men, so that they may see your good works and give glory to your Father who is in heaven.” (Matthew 5:16)',
 assess='Ask students to present their mission statement and one commitment.'),
'12-8-2': dict(
 idea='A rule of life is a realistic plan for daily and weekly practices that keep a person close to God: Mass, Confession, daily prayer, Scripture, service, rest, and community. It brings structure so love can grow.',
 hook='Read Luke 18:1: “Jesus told them a parable to the effect that they ought always to pray and not lose heart.” Ask: “What helps you keep a habit?”',
 activity='“Create a rule of life” (45 min). Students draft a rule with daily (Morning Offering, Scripture, Angelus or short prayer, Examen), weekly (Sunday Mass, a service act, a rest day), monthly (Confession, a retreat or spiritual reading), yearly (a retreat). They share it with a friend or mentor and set a review date.',
 teach=['A rule of life gives order and consistency to the Christian life, as in religious orders and in lay associations (CCC 2030). It should be realistic, flexible and revisable.',
        'Prayer is essential: “pray constantly” (1 Thessalonians 5:17; CCC 2742–2745). The sacraments, especially Eucharist and Penance, nourish. Scripture forms mind and heart.',
        'Acts of service and rest keep the rule balanced. Spiritual direction helps keep it honest.',
        'Teach perseverance: relapse and restart are normal. Grace comes before and after effort.'],
 misc=['“A rule of life is legalism.”', '“Anything less than an hour of prayer a day doesn’t count.”'],
 mem='“Pray without ceasing.” (1 Thessalonians 5:17)',
 assess='Ask students to present their rule and explain how it supports love of God and neighbor.'),
'12-8-3': dict(
 idea='“Go therefore and make disciples of all nations.” As graduates and young adults, students are sent. The Church’s mission is carried out by missionary disciples who witness with their lives and words, wherever they go.',
 hook='Read Matthew 28:19–20. Ask: “What are the verbs? What is the promise?” Go, make disciples, baptize, teach; and “I am with you always.”',
 activity='“Commissioning” (45 min). Hold a simple commissioning rite: reading, a moment where each student names their commitment aloud, a blessing, the Sending Forth, a prayer. Provide a symbolic gift (a cross, a small Bible, a prayer card). Close with a hymn.',
 teach=['The Church is missionary by nature (CCC 849–851; Ad Gentes 2). Evangelization includes witness of life, proclamation, dialogue, works of charity (CCC 2044).',
        'Pope Francis (Evangelii Gaudium) speaks of “missionary disciples.” Every Christian is both disciple and missionary.',
        'Go with humility, joy, courage and openness. The Spirit goes before.',
        'Remain connected to the Church community: parish, campus ministry, young adult groups.'],
 misc=['“Mission is only for those who go overseas.”', '“Faith is private.”'],
 mem='“Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit… and behold, I am with you always.” (Matthew 28:19–20)',
 assess='Ask students to state their commitments to mission after graduation.'),
'12-9-1': dict(
 idea='The Creed is the summary of the faith. Reviewing it article by article helps students see how doctrine fits together and how it shapes life. Each student writes a commentary on the Creed in their own words.',
 hook='Read Romans 10:9. Ask: “What do you confess with your lips? What do you believe in your heart?”',
 activity='“Write my own creed commentary” (50 min). Students choose two articles (e.g., “maker of heaven and earth”; “he descended to the dead”; “communion of saints”) and write a half-page each: what it says, Scripture, a Catechism reference, what difference it makes to my life. Recite the Creed together.',
 teach=['The Creed organizes the faith: Father, Son, Holy Spirit and the Church (CCC 185–197). It is both a summary of faith and a personal profession.',
        'The Creed has twelve articles in the Apostles’ Creed, traditionally attributed to the apostles (CCC 194). The Nicene Creed adds precision on Christ’s divinity and the Spirit.',
        'Doctrine is connected to prayer and morals; lex orandi, lex credendi, lex vivendi.',
        'Encourage students to pray the Creed regularly, e.g., at Mass, with attention.'],
 misc=['“The Creed is an old formula with no relevance.”', '“I can pick which articles to believe.”'],
 mem='“If you confess with your lips that Jesus is Lord and believe in your heart that God raised him from the dead, you will be saved.” (Romans 10:9)',
 assess='Ask students to share one article of the Creed and what it means for their life.'),
'12-9-2': dict(
 idea='This lesson integrates the sacraments and the moral life: grace received in the sacraments strengthens the life of love lived in the commandments and Beatitudes. Students present what they have learned and its coherence.',
 hook='Read Matthew 22:37–40. Ask: “How do the sacraments help us live these two commands?”',
 activity='“Presentation” (50 min). In groups, students prepare a short presentation connecting a sacrament to a moral theme (Eucharist and justice; Penance and truth; Matrimony and chastity; Orders and service). They use Scripture, Catechism sections and a real-life example. Peers give feedback.',
 teach=['The sacraments are the principal means of grace; the moral life is the response (CCC 1210–1211, 1716, 2052). Grace heals and elevates, the commandments and Beatitudes direct.',
        'The Catechism has four pillars: Creed, sacraments, commandments, prayer. They interlock: what we believe, celebrate, live and pray.',
        'Sacramental grace is not a license but a power: the Eucharist makes us one body and sends us.',
        'Encourage students to find the thread through the whole course.'],
 misc=['“Morality and sacraments are separate topics.”', '“Grace makes obedience unnecessary.”'],
 mem='“You shall love the Lord your God… and your neighbor as yourself.” (Matthew 22:37–39)',
 assess='Ask students to explain how a sacrament strengthens a moral virtue with a concrete example.'),
'12-9-3': dict(
 idea='The capstone is a final project in which students share what they have learned, how it has changed them and how they plan to live it, including a prayer. It is a chance to take ownership of faith as adults.',
 hook='Read Philippians 3:12–14. Ask: “What does Paul mean by pressing on toward the goal?” Ask students what they hope to carry forward.',
 activity='“Capstone project” (a week of preparation; 60 min sessions). Each student creates a project: a written reflection, a video, a service initiative, an artwork, or a teaching session. It includes: three key insights, one challenge or question, a plan for next steps and a prayer. Present to the class or parish, with a closing blessing.',
 teach=['A capstone integrates learning, as in Catechism’s structure (CCC 2030). It invites personal appropriation: “faith seeking understanding.”',
        'Include a plan: sacraments, service, study, community, mentor. A plan needs accountability.',
        'The prayer is part of the work: thanksgiving, petition, offering.',
        'Celebrate: an agape meal or a Mass of thanksgiving would be fitting.'],
 misc=['“The end of the course is the end of formation.”', '“A capstone is just another assignment.”'],
 mem='“Forgetting what lies behind and straining forward to what lies ahead, I press on toward the goal.” (Philippians 3:13–14)',
 assess='Evaluate capstones using a rubric: accuracy, depth, personal engagement, concrete plan and prayer.'),
}
